A sound stream at another rate than the Mac's output gets a Mac pull of one period: patch 0009's buffer size is counted in device frames.

This repository's patch to winecoreaudio (version 1), on top of 0009 (upstream's period frame size), to be offered
to WineHQ. It is mavrik's own: upstream master (checked 2026-10-30) has 0009's code unchanged.

0009 sets the Mac's I/O buffer (kAudioDevicePropertyBufferFrameSize) to the stream's period so the Mac pulls one
period at a time. But it computes the period in the stream's frames (at the format's nSamplesPerSec) and hands that
number to the device, whose buffer size and its allowed range count frames at the device's nominal rate. A shared
stream at another rate than the device (a 48 kHz stream on a 44.1 kHz output, or the reverse) therefore got a pull
longer or shorter than its period: 557 frames for a 48 kHz stream's 11.6 ms period became 557 device frames, 12.6
ms at 44.1 kHz. A pull longer than the refill is the starving pattern 0009 exists to stop. Apple's output unit
converts the stream's rate to the device's, so the stream's own format stays as it was.

Now the period is converted to device frames at the device's nominal rate (0009's get_device_sample_rate; the
stream's own rate if that read fails), clamped to the device's range there, and set as the device's buffer size;
the stream's period in its own frames is computed back from it, so a clamp still shows in the period Wine reports.
At the same rate nothing changes.

Checked (mavrik S217 lab): applies after patches 0001 to 0037 with no fuzz; winecoreaudio builds with the engine's
toolchain with no new warning. Its effect on underruns rides mavrik r11's sound run and no-regression run.

diff --git a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
index 43310e3..fbf438d 100644
--- a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
+++ b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
@@ -811,7 +811,7 @@ static HRESULT get_device_sample_rate(AudioDeviceID dev_id, EDataFlow flow,
 static NTSTATUS unix_create_stream(void *args)
 {
     struct create_stream_params *params = args;
-    unsigned int min_period_frames, max_period_frames;
+    unsigned int min_period_frames, max_period_frames, dev_period_frames, dev_rate;
     struct coreaudio_stream *stream;
     AURenderCallbackStruct input;
     OSStatus sc;
@@ -847,9 +847,18 @@ static NTSTATUS unix_create_stream(void *args)
                                                               &max_period_frames)))
         goto end;
 
-    stream->period_frames = max(min_period_frames, min(stream->period_frames, max_period_frames));
+    /* mavrik: the device's buffer size and its range count device frames, at the device's
+     * rate, while period_frames counts the stream's. Convert, so that a stream at another
+     * rate gets a Mac pull of one period, not of period_frames device frames (a 48 kHz
+     * stream on a 44.1 kHz device pulled 12.6 ms for an 11.6 ms period, more than the
+     * game refills). The same rate changes nothing. */
+    if (FAILED(get_device_sample_rate(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, &dev_rate)) || !dev_rate)
+        dev_rate = stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec;
+    dev_period_frames = muldiv(stream->period_frames, dev_rate, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec);
+    dev_period_frames = max(min_period_frames, min(dev_period_frames, max_period_frames));
+    stream->period_frames = max(1, muldiv(dev_period_frames, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec, dev_rate));
     stream->period = muldiv(stream->period_frames, 10000000, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec);
-    if (FAILED(params->result = set_device_period_frame_size(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, stream->period_frames)))
+    if (FAILED(params->result = set_device_period_frame_size(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, dev_period_frames)))
         goto end;
 
     stream->bufsize_frames = muldiv(params->duration, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec, 10000000);
